1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:07,670
This video shows you how to send some of a source's records through a pipeline of their own.

2
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:10,316
You're where Getting started ended.

3
00:00:10,316 --> 00:00:16,842
One route sends every record from tutorial-in through tutorial-in-parse to tutorial-out.

4
00:00:16,842 --> 00:00:21,230
The records come from two services, checkout and search.

5
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:24,890
Checkout records should get a pipeline of their own.

6
00:00:24,890 --> 00:00:27,190
Add a route, from tutorial-in.

7
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:31,182
The card now draws every route that reads tutorial-in.

8
00:00:31,182 --> 00:00:34,030
The new route sits beside the first.

9
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:38,402
Give the new route a condition, so it takes only checkout records.

10
00:00:38,402 --> 00:00:43,350
A route's condition reads the raw record, before any pipeline parses it.

11
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:46,578
Make a new pipeline for it, and capture a sample.

12
00:00:46,578 --> 00:00:50,110
The capture keeps only the records the route takes.

13
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:53,884
Start it, and stop it after a few records.

14
00:00:55,640 --> 00:01:00,354
Save them as a sample called checkout, to check the routes with later.

15
00:01:00,354 --> 00:01:05,533
A sample is saved on a branch, so make a new branch, checkout-route.

16
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:09,605
Call the pipeline checkout-parse.

17
00:01:09,605 --> 00:01:13,050
smeltr reads each record as JSON.

18
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:20,014
Add an Eval step that labels these records smeltr.checkout.

19
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:29,756
Save the pipeline.

20
00:01:29,756 --> 00:01:31,870
It goes on the same branch.

21
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:37,670
Back on the route, send checkout-parse to a new Kafka destination, checkout-out.

22
00:01:37,670 --> 00:01:41,073
It writes to the topic smeltr-checkout.

23
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:52,009
Save the route.

24
00:01:52,880 --> 00:01:56,479
Before you deploy, route the checkout sample.

25
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:06,527
Each checkout record goes to both destinations.

26
00:02:06,527 --> 00:02:07,960
Routes have no order.

27
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:11,372
Every route whose condition matches a record runs,

28
00:02:11,372 --> 00:02:15,870
and the first route has no condition, so it takes every record.

29
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:18,903
To send each record through only one pipeline,

30
00:02:18,903 --> 00:02:22,356
give the first route the opposite condition.

31
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:28,057
Route the sample again.

32
00:02:28,057 --> 00:02:32,213
Now checkout records go only to checkout-out.

33
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:36,626
Open the branch for review, and deploy it.

34
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:45,864
Check on learning.

35
00:02:46,064 --> 00:02:48,559
Two routes match the same record.

36
00:02:48,559 --> 00:02:50,898
Which pipeline runs it?

37
00:02:55,298 --> 00:02:55,669
Both.

38
00:02:55,669 --> 00:02:57,228
Routes have no order.

39
00:02:57,228 --> 00:03:04,213
To send a record through only one, give the routes conditions that exclude each other.

40
00:03:05,113 --> 00:03:09,421
Next, choose destinations by what a record holds.
